setup = Game Setup
    .details =
    The game is played with 2, 3, or 4 players. Each player runs an independent video game company.
            Place the 5 Activity cards face up in the center of the table so all players can access them.
            Create the pile of Earnings cards face down.
            Each player receives an "ADVERGAME Product Team" card placed on the table in front of them. This card represents the initial state of the company: a single product team responsible for creating advergames.
            Shuffle the other "Product Team", "Agile Maturity", and "Agile Value" cards to form a common draw pile. Each player draws 4 cards. These cards are kept in hand and constitute the company's initial potential.
            One of the players is designated to receive the "Game Leader" card.

goal = Goal of the Game
    .details =
    The winning player is the one whose company has the most points at the end of the game.
            A company's score is the sum of the points from the cards played during the game.

start-round = How a Round Works
    .details =
    The player with the "Game Leader" card chooses an activity, takes the corresponding card, and places it in front of them.
            All players then perform the chosen activity, starting with the player who selected it.
            The next player chooses a new activity from the remaining four, and the process repeats until each player has chosen an activity.

activities = How an Activity Works

activities-detail = Each Activity card describes the precise flow of the associated action:
    .item1 = CONFERENCE. All players increase their potential by attending a conference: each draws two cards and keeps one; the second is discarded. The initiating player draws 5 cards and discards 4 of them.
    .item2 = DEVELOPMENT. Each company can develop only one product. To do this, the player must use a potential card by placing it, face down, on one of their product teams. The initiating player can perform this action twice, applying it to two different product teams. Note: A product team cannot develop a second product as long as the current product has not been deployed.
    .item3 = DEPLOYMENT. Flip the top card of the Earnings pile. Each company can deploy only one product. The deployed product card is then discarded, and the player draws the number of cards indicated on the Earnings card based on the concerned product team. The initiating player can deploy products with two different product teams. At the end of this activity, shuffle the Earnings cards to recreate a face-down pile.
    .item4 = RETROSPECTIVE. Each player can improve their company by playing an improvement card from their potential. To validate the improvement, the player must discard a number of cards equal to the cost indicated on the card. If they do not have enough cards, they must choose another improvement or refrain. For the initiating player, the cost is reduced by one card. With the exception of product teams, whose number is not limited, it is not possible to play the same improvement twice to cumulate its effects.
    .item5 = COACH. The player who chose this activity—and only them—benefits from a coach and draws an additional card to add to their potential.

end-round = End of Round
    .details =
    After all chosen activities have been performed, the round ends. The "Game Leader" card is passed to the next player. Each player must keep a maximum of 6 potential cards in hand; excess cards must be discarded.

end-game = End of Game
    .details =
    The game ends after 48 activities.
    Each player totals their company's points (sum of points from played cards) to determine the winner.
    The number of rounds is adjusted based on the number of players: 12 rounds for 4 players, 16 rounds for 3 players, 24 rounds for 2 players.

    When there is not enough time for a full game, shorter games can be played in 36 activities (9 rounds for 4 players, 12 rounds for 3 players, 18 rounds for 2 players) or 24 activities (6 rounds for 4 players, 8 rounds for 3 players, 12 rounds for 2 players).

addendum1 = Impact of Agile Maturity
    .details =
    During a retrospective, improvements related to agile maturity influence both the score and the flow of activities.
            Each maturity card specifies its effect as well as the icon corresponding to the concerned activity.
            For example, the "Clean Code" card allows the DEVELOPMENT action to be performed twice instead of once, or even three times if the player initiates the activity.
            A maturity improvement can only be implemented once in the company, its effect being permanent upon activation.

addendum2 = Impact of Agile Values
    .details =
    Each adopted value allows, at the end of the round, to keep one additional potential card (beyond the maximum of 6).
            At the end of the game, the score obtained for each value depends on the total number of values adopted by the company: 1 value: 1 point, 2 values: 2 points each (for a total of 4 points), 3 values: 3 points each (for a total of 9 points). And so on.
